  "root_cause": "execute_transfer function + sender.balance/receiver.balance variables + mixing observability (ledger.record_*/audit.emit_*) calls with business logic caused partial side-effects: when an exception is raised during the credit phase the sender's balance was already debited and no rollback occurred, leaving balances and recorded logs inconsistent.",
  "fix_strategy": "Extract the pure business logic into a new helper _core_transfer(sender, receiver, amount) that performs validation and updates balances atomically (with an explicit rollback of the debit if the credit phase fails). Move all ledger and audit calls into the execute_transfer wrapper so they are only executed before/after the core transfer. The wrapper records a pending attempt, invokes the core transfer, and on success records debit/credit, marks the attempt completed and emits the transfer event; on failure it marks the attempt failed and emits a failure alert. Observability calls are wrapped in try/except to avoid leaving the core state inconsistent if an observability call raises.",
  "risk_check": "Most likely remaining failure mode: observability functions (ledger.record_* or audit.emit_*) could raise exceptions after the business transfer completed (or while marking pending/failed) which would leave the balances correct but the logs/alerts inconsistent or cause additional exceptions to propagate. I mitigated this by catching exceptions around observability calls and attempting to emit a failure alert when possible; those emit calls are also guarded. This is a pragmatic mitigation — fully robust handling would require durable, transactional logging or retrying observability operations, which is out of scope here.",
